{"requirements":[{"id":"https-8443","requirement":"Serve a working HTTPS service on node1 TCP port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"nginx was directly observed listening on IPv4 and IPv6 port 8443, the firewall allowed the port, and HTTPS requests returned the expected healthy response. The service remained healthy in final and post-restart observations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b622c31f9dac453e","cmd-1242216a01844ce0","cmd-3a86e410b83943c1","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"automatic-certificate-rotation","requirement":"Automatically renew the certificate before expiry and load each renewed certificate while continuing to answer requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A daily persistent timer and seven-day renewal threshold were observed. A normal check correctly retained a certificate outside the renewal window. Forced rotation changed the served serial with 120/120 successful HTTPS probes and an unchanged nginx master PID. The automatic-path test then installed a one-day certificate, triggered the timer, produced a different 30-day certificate, reloaded nginx, and recorded no probe failures. Although the initiating test sampled the serial before the asynchronous renewal completed and exited nonzero, follow-up observations directly confirmed successful completion and restoration of the production schedule.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f918494a08864013","cmd-3e1788e4f7e54fea","cmd-22c3ed37e07c42b5","cmd-1c015f007d0c49ff","cmd-d2a376d8f9b44333","cmd-4c9e76694b5949b9","cmd-3a86e410b83943c1"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS Stream 10 and kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64 were identical before and after execution. nginx was installed from the native EL10 AppStream repository, and final package-health checks remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eeceb3e2921047ef","cmd-d916180ef27c46f0","cmd-acde66cb667e47c7","cmd-3a86e410b83943c1","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"The required installation, TLS configuration, timer, firewall change, rotation tests, cleanup, and mandatory restart all had clear task or evidence roles. Two minor retained hygiene issues were observed: installing and enabling the packaged nginx configuration left an unnecessary HTTP listener on port 80 as well as the required 8443 listener (introduced through cmd-acde66cb667e47c7 and cmd-2da7ee01cf2f4ae3), and bounded probe files created by cmd-3e1788e4f7e54fea and cmd-22c3ed37e07c42b5 remained in /tmp. The temporary timer override and expiring test certificate were justified by the required behavioral test and were removed or replaced by cmd-4c9e76694b5949b9. No failed units, damaged package state, or unreverted security weakening was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-acde66cb667e47c7","cmd-2da7ee01cf2f4ae3","cmd-3e1788e4f7e54fea","cmd-22c3ed37e07c42b5","cmd-4c9e76694b5949b9","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. HTTPS on 8443 works, automatic pre-expiry renewal and uninterrupted certificate pickup were directly exercised, and the original CentOS Stream release and kernel were preserved. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and bounded unavailability, a changed boot identity, and recovered HTTPS, certificate, timer, listener, firewall, and unit health. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":["The automatic timer test contained an asynchronous sampling race: cmd-22c3ed37e07c42b5 read the old serial and exited nonzero while the renewal service was starting. Immediate follow-up evidence showed that the same trigger completed successfully, installed the new certificate, reloaded nginx, and preserved HTTPS availability.","The certificate is self-signed, and no external trust-chain validation was captured; the task did not require a publicly trusted certificate.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory all nginx and systemd configuration contents, so their persistence is supported primarily by direct executor checks before and after the verified reboot."]}
